# Test Directory Layout

Test code never lives inside production source files. Put every test in the
dedicated test directory for its layer, so `src/` stays free of test modules
and test helpers.

## Rust (`crates/<crate>/`)

- Tests go in `crates/<crate>/tests/*.rs` (one file per area, e.g. `tests/audit_client_id.rs`).
- Do **not** add `#[cfg(test)] mod tests { ... }` blocks inside `src/*.rs`.
- Do **not** create `src/**/tests/` subdirectories.
- Integration tests can only reach `pub` items. If a helper is only public
  for testing, say so in its doc comment (`/// Exposed for tests; ...`) and
  keep its surface minimal.
- DB-backed tests use the shared harness in `crates/signet/tests/common/` (create if absent).

```rust
// ❌ BAD — inline test module in crates/signet/src/audit.rs
#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    #[test]
    fn parses_return_to() { ... }
}

// ✅ GOOD — crates/signet/tests/audit_client_id.rs
use signet::audit::resolve_audit_client_id_contract;

#[test]
fn parses_return_to() { ... }
```

## Frontend (`dashboard/`)

- Tests go in `dashboard/tests/` with the `*.spec.ts` suffix (e.g. `tests/overview.spec.ts`).
- Do **not** colocate `*.spec.ts` / `*.test.ts` files next to components or views.
- Test-only fixtures and helpers live under `dashboard/tests/fixtures/`.

## Running

- Rust: `cargo test --workspace` (picks up every `tests/` target).
- Frontend: `npm run test` in `dashboard/` (wire up the script when adding the first spec).
